/-!
# Pressure, the Bowen zero, and a certified exponent atlas for the model system

`Z32/EntropyCert.lean` brackets `φ_model([0,ℓ))` — the growth rate of the carry language of the
model system of the base `3/2` on an anchored window — to eight decimals, by an exact finite type
graph.  This file puts the thermodynamic layer on top: the pressure `P(t)` of the geometric
potential, its unique zero (the **Bowen zero**), and the two exponent readings the zero carries.

## The pressure is affine, and that is a theorem

The branches of the model system are `y ↦ (p·y − s)/q`: affine, all with the same slope `p/q`
(`modelBranch_sub`).  So `|T'|` is a *constant*, the geometric potential `−t·log|T'|` is the
constant `−t·log(p/q)`, its Birkhoff sum over a length-`k` word is `−t·k·log(p/q)`, and the
partition function degenerates to a product:

`Z_k(t) = ∑_{w ∈ modelLang k} exp(S_k φ_t) = #modelLang(k) · exp(−t·k·log(p/q))`.

Hence `modelPressure_eq`:

`P(t) = φ_model(U) − t·log(p/q)`,   for every real `t`.

This is not a modelling shortcut, it is what a single-slope conformal repeller does; and it is
better news than the two-sided enclosure at each rational `t` that was planned for, because *one*
certificate now encloses the whole pressure function at once, on both sides, at every real `t`
(`TypeCert.modelPressure_mem_Icc`).  At the operator level the same degeneracy is
`TypeCert.psum_eq_card_modelLang`: weighting every edge of the type graph by `θ` multiplies the
transfer operator's output by exactly `θ^k`, so the leading eigenvalue is `θ·λ` and nothing about
the spectrum is new.  The general weighted certificates that make this a theorem rather than an
identification live in `ForMathlib/Combinatorics/PathGrowth.lean` (`psum`, `psum_le`, `le_psum`);
they are what a non-affine variant would consume.

## Two exponents, one number

`modelExponent p q U r = φ_model(U) / log r` is the exponent of the language count in base `r`.
Two bases matter here and they are related by a constant:

* `modelBowen U = modelExponent 3 2 U (3/2)` — the **Bowen zero**, the unique `t` with `P(t) = 0`.
  This is the conformal exponent: a depth-`k` cylinder has diameter `(2/3)^k` times its image's
  (`abs_back_sub`), so this is the exponent Bowen's formula attaches to the survivor set.
* `flattoExponent U = modelExponent 3 2 U 2` — the **dyadic counting exponent**, the exponent in
  Flatto's `#Z(x) ≤ 3·x^s` at the Z-cell.

They differ by the factor `log₂(3/2)` (`flattoExponent_eq`), and at the Z-cell the first is
*exactly 1* (`modelBowen_zCell`) while the second is *exactly* `log₂(3/2) = 0.5849…`
(`flattoExponent_zCell`).  That is plan-B10's F2 wall in dimension language: the conformal exponent
of the model saturates at the top of the window family, so all the information sits in the dyadic
reading — and the `0.585` of the wall and the `0.585` of Table C's `s₀` are literally the same
number, obtained here from `Z32.Flatto.phiModel_zCell`.

## No logarithm reaches the kernel

Every bound below is a rational power comparison.  For `r, x > 1`,

`n/d ≤ log x / log r  ⟺  r^n ≤ x^d`   (`div_le_log_div_log`, `log_div_log_le_div`),

so a seven-decimal bracket on an exponent is two integer inequalities, discharged by `norm_num`
over GMP-accelerated numerals.  Nothing rounds, nothing floats, and no interval-arithmetic library
is involved.

## Main results

* `modelPressure`, `modelPressure_eq` — the pressure and its affine closed form.
* `strictAnti_modelPressure`, `modelBowen_mem_Icc` — strict monotonicity in `t`, hence a unique
  zero and the **monotone interval bracketing**: `0 ≤ P(t₁)` and `P(t₂) ≤ 0` give
  `t₁ ≤ t* ≤ t₂`.
* `TypeCert.modelPressure_mem_Icc` — `P_lo(t) ≤ P(t) ≤ P_hi(t)` at every real `t`, from one
  certificate.
* `TypeCert.modelPressure_nonneg_of_pow`, `TypeCert.modelPressure_nonpos_of_pow` — the sign of the
  pressure at a rational `t`, decided by integer arithmetic; and
  `TypeCert.modelBowen_mem_Icc_of_pow`, which brackets the Bowen zero by running the two.
* `bowen_band_mem_Icc` and its six siblings — the atlas, to seven decimals.
* `modelBowen_zCell`, `flattoExponent_zCell`, `modelPressure_zCell` — the Z-cell endpoint, exact.

## The atlas (plan-B10 WP4′ / report2-weyl.html B5)

Generated by `Z32/pressure.py` from the certificates of `Z32/entcert.py`, independently re-checked
by `Z32/verify_entcert.py`.  Both columns are certified in this file by the two power comparisons
shown for the flagship row.

```
   ℓ        φ_model([0,ℓ))   Bowen zero t*(ℓ)   dyadic s₀(ℓ)
  2/5         0.2872663         0.7084860         0.4144378
  13/32       0.3059170         0.7544841         0.4413449
  5/12        0.3222846         0.7948517         0.4649584   ← flagship
  7/16        0.3361212         0.8289769         0.4849204
  9/20        0.3556157         0.8770561         0.5130449
  11/24       0.3659409         0.9025214         0.5279411
  23/48       0.3822451         0.9427324         0.5514631
  1/2         0.4054651         1                 0.5849625   = log₂(3/2), both exact
```

Each entry is the midpoint of the certified interval, rounded to seven decimals; the interval
itself is the `Set.Icc` of the corresponding theorem and is narrower than `2 · 10⁻⁷` in every row.
Both exponent columns are monotone in `ℓ` and approach their Z-cell values; the Bowen column
reaching `1` is the saturation described above, and is why the plan's `δ < 0.585` target lives in
the third column, not the second.

**Cost.**  The pressure layer adds no `decide` at all: it reuses the seven certificates of WP3
verbatim, and its own kernel work is numeral exponentiation.  Measured on this machine at
`LEAN_NUM_THREADS=3`: the twenty-eight power comparisons of the atlas — integers of up to
`51 202` decimal digits — cost `0.27 s` in isolation (`1.51 s` against a `1.24 s` import
baseline), and the whole file elaborates in `2.3 s` over its own `1.6 s` import baseline.

## What is *not* claimed

* **No Hausdorff-dimension statement.**  `modelBowen` is the zero of the pressure, and the file
  says so; identifying it with `dimH` of the survivor set needs Bowen's formula, which Mathlib does
  not have in any form.  The geometry is in place — `abs_back_sub` says the inverse branches
  contract by exactly `q/p`, and for `ℓ < 1/2` the type set is finite so the cylinder-to-image
  ratio is bounded away from `0` and `∞`, making the survivor set a strongly separated
  graph-directed self-similar set of constant ratio — but the measure-theoretic half is not
  formalised here.  See `§ Plan corrections` item 6.
* Nothing here is about genuine Z-numbers, for the reasons `Z32/EntropyCert.lean` records: this is
  the entropy, and now the pressure, of the **carry model**.
* `flattoExponent U` is *defined* for every window, but the counting theorem behind the name
  (`Z32.Flatto.card_zNumbers_le`) exists only at the Z-cell.  For `ℓ < 1/2` the number is an
  exponent of the model language in base `2`, nothing more.

## Plan corrections recorded (for plan-B10 WP7)

1. **The `t`-weighted grid is not a grid.**  §8-O2 and Table E's B5 row price "a weighted
   extension of `PathGrowth`'s two vector-certificate lemmas" plus "pressure enclosures at rational
   `t`".  The extension is delivered (`PathGrowth.psum` and its two certificates, which are
   *cheaper* than the unweighted ones: the lower bound needs no determinism and neither needs
   extreme-weight constants).  But for this system the weights are constant, so the enclosure at
   one `t` gives the enclosure at all of them: `modelPressure_eq` is a closed form, not a grid.
2. **Trigger (j) does not bind.**  The gate watches the `t`-weighted certificates staying inside
   WP3's `D ≲ 20`-digit envelope.  `t`-weighting never touches `TypeCert.ok`; the seven WP3
   certificates are reused unchanged and the new kernel work is numeral exponentiation, which is
   GMP-accelerated — `51 202`-digit comparisons cost milliseconds.  B5 holds at `0.85–0.95` and
   the envelope clause is inapplicable, not merely satisfied.
3. **No ball arithmetic anywhere.**  The plan's original B10 sketch wanted "a rigorous
   ball-arithmetic (Arb) enclosure"; WP3 retired that for the eigenvalue, and the equivalence
   `n/d ≤ log x / log r ⟺ r^n ≤ x^d` retires it for the pressure and the exponents too.
4. **The two `0.585`s are one number, and the Bowen zero at the Z-cell is `1`.**  WP5's planned
   cross-reference ("the wall at depth `0.585 n` and this `0.585` are the same number seen twice")
   is `flattoExponent_zCell` together with `modelBowen_zCell`: the conformal exponent saturates at
   `1`, and `s₀ = t* · log₂(3/2)`.
5. **B-portfolio item 4's `TH/Pressure/Pressure.lean` is this file**, `Z32/Pressure.lean`: its
   content is the Z32 model system, it consumes two Z32 files, and `TH/` is not an Extract corpus
   root.
6. **Named next step.**  `dimH (survivor set) = modelBowen` — Bowen's formula for a strongly
   separated graph-directed self-similar set of constant ratio.  Mathlib has `dimH` and the
   Hausdorff measures but no Moran-type theorem; the covering (upper) half is reachable from
   `abs_back_sub` plus `Metric.hausdorffMeasure`, the mass-distribution (lower) half is a session
   of its own.  Nothing else in B5 depends on it, which is why it is recorded rather than
   attempted here.

## References

* R. Bowen, *Hausdorff dimension of quasi-circles*, Publ. IHÉS **50** (1979) — the zero of the
  pressure; W. Parry and M. Pollicott, *Zeta functions and the periodic orbit structure of
